at the peak of covet in march  anxiety-related searches hit a record  high on google  as people were searching for answers on  how to handle a year filled with  confusion  turmoil and grief i sat down with people  who study wellness to find out what that  means and why focusing on your  well-being  is more crucial now than ever  nestled in the bucolic hills of new  england sits the mayflower in the newest  outpost of the well  a new york city-based center focused on  wellness and relaxation  rebecca park is the co-founder and ceo  we know how healing nature can be and so  to create an opportunity for guests to  really go deep on their journey  through overnight retreats and  experiences some of the signature  treatments we've created  are intentionally designed to take place  in nature treatments like this  an outdoor massage under a canopy of  trees  we created a treatment where the massage  table is warmed with infrared heat  and so you're laying on the table  getting this profound craniosacral  treatment  while looking up at the trees so  creating these kind of magical moments  and experiences for people  other services at the well range from 50  to 600  offerings vary from yoga and meditation  classes  to acupuncture to one-on-one sessions  with a health coach  park was first inspired to create what  she calls an ecosystem for wellness  after working on a trading desk during  the 2008 financial crisis  i found that it was very challenging to  make time  for wellness and really just to  prioritize it and then  even when i did attempt to say i need to  find a yoga studio or i need to figure  out how to sleep more  it was just very disparate there were so  many amazing wellness  experiences and brands that were coming  to life in new york that everything was  fragmented  the well aims to change that by offering  all  wellness related services under one roof  centers like these represent the newest  frontier in the booming 4.5  trillion dollar global wellness economy  craig reed is president and ceo of  auberge resorts  parent company to the mayflower inn he  says the hospitality industry is  evolving  factoring wellness into the business  people  are constantly concerned about their  physical health their emotional health  we in our resorts we sort of see them as  havens  as retreats where people can go and  escape and  renew and be re-energized and so  wellness for us  in a place like this is about uh sort of  supporting that journey of renewal  and encouragement but rebecca park  acknowledges  wellness can feel out of reach for many  you're talking about  meditation and yoga and things that may  not be part of their own  routines or lies what would you say to  people who  look at this and say well that's  wellness for the well-off  i think it's a fair point and i think  it's a valid question and i think it's  something that we need to talk about  more  so i think first off i think as a  wellness company  thought leaders in the wellness industry  have a responsibility to advocate for  policy and systemic change  that makes wellness accessible to more  people psychiatrist dr marquita wills  says wellness is critical for everyone  i'd argue that it's it's not a luxury  in fact in times like these during high  stress times  it's actually a necessity because the  thing of the matter is  if we don't take care of ourselves it  comes out it comes out in other ways  it'll come out with weight gain it'll  come out with inability to sleep it'll  come out with being  argumentative and irritable at work so  we got to take charge of it so that we  can be our best selves and live our best  lives  and dr wills says self-care doesn't have  to cost a lot  you can buy epsom salt throw it in your  tub  and create your own spa with a candle at  home and so building those restorative  experiences into  our lives are so important especially  now  as for the well the pandemic forced its  new york city location to temporarily  shut its doors  but provided an opportunity to introduce  the company's services to a wider  audience  we very quickly pivoted and brought all  of our services online  we created a telehealth platform we  started live streaming our fitness  classes  we started offering free meditation led  by one of our teachers  now park hopes more people will see why  wellness matters  our mental and emotional well-being is  certainly being challenged right now you  know we were experiencing a mental  health crisis in america  prior to copin 19. the amount of stress  and anxiety that people are experiencing  on a day-to-day basis  is really challenging and daunting so i  think it's critical that we come  together  as individuals as businesses as a  collective society and help each other  heal right now  all right so we should mention that we  shot some of this before the pandemic  before  social distancing hence some of the  footage that you saw there but  dr wills says it's really important for  people to understand this is for  everyone people of all backgrounds and  that sleep  dr will said should be a priority we all  know how  sleep can affect us but really there's  just a myriad of body processes that are  affected when you don't get enough sleep  um she says also just meditate if you  can which sounds like a tall order for a  lot of people  they don't like to do that but just even  10 minutes take a breath yes  but i love the epsom salt and a candle  that's right  when all else fails that's right
 

